.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt NOQTIS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm noqtis
.Nd post-quantum signatures, KEMs and sealed capsules of files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar args
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl -help
.Nm
.Fl V | Fl -version
.Nm
.Cm help
.Op Ar command
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
records the exact files a result came from, signs that record with a
post-quantum signature (ML-DSA, FIPS 204, or SLH-DSA, FIPS 205) and checks
such records later.
It also signs and verifies single files, runs the ML-KEM (FIPS 203) and
HQC key encapsulation mechanisms, and prints and checks file digests.
It is a thin tool over the public C interface of
.Xr noqtis 3
and calls nothing else.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width "version"
.It Cm keygen
Make a key pair.
See
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1 .
.It Cm sign
Sign a file.
See
.Xr noqtis-sign 1 .
.It Cm verify
Check a signature.
See
.Xr noqtis-verify 1 .
.It Cm enc
Encapsulate a shared secret to a KEM public key.
See
.Xr noqtis-enc 1 .
.It Cm dec
Decapsulate a shared secret with a KEM secret key.
See
.Xr noqtis-dec 1 .
.It Cm box
Seal a file of any size to an ML-KEM public key, in chunks.
See
.Xr noqtis-box 1 .
.It Cm unbox
Open a file sealed with
.Cm box .
See
.Xr noqtis-unbox 1 .
.It Cm hash
Print or check file digests in the format of
.Xr sha256sum 1 .
See
.Xr noqtis-hash 1 .
.It Cm seal
Seal a folder: write
.Pa noqtis.json
and
.Pa noqtis.sig
into it.
See
.Xr noqtis-seal 1 .
.It Cm check
Check a sealed folder against its seal.
See
.Xr noqtis-check 1 .
.It Cm prove
Prove that some files are in a format 2 capsule, without the others.
See
.Xr noqtis-prove 1 .
.It Cm chain
Check an append-only chain of capsules, each sealed with the one before
as its input, and give its head.
See
.Xr noqtis-chain 1 .
.It Cm export
The capsule as in-toto (signed in DSSE), RO-Crate, BagIt or a bundle
(one file that
.Cm check
reads).
See
.Xr noqtis-export 1 .
.It Cm pack
Start, build and check a reproducibility bundle: a capsule with its
environment and lock files recorded, shipped as one file.
See
.Xr noqtis-pack 1 .
.It Cm data
Make, show and check a capsule's data record,
.Pa noqtis.data.json
.Pq Xr noqtis-data 5 .
See
.Xr noqtis-data 1 .
.It Cm schema
Pin and check the schema of CSV files.
See
.Xr noqtis-schema 1 .
.It Cm src
Fetch the sources of a capsule's data record, and say if they changed.
See
.Xr noqtis-src 1 .
.It Cm drift
Summarise CSV files, and say later whether they drifted.
See
.Xr noqtis-drift 1 .
.It Cm chunk
Record the chunk digests of a large file, and check a piece of it
alone.
See
.Xr noqtis-chunk 1 .
.It Cm env
Record the environment of a run in the data record.
See
.Xr noqtis-env 1 .
.It Cm commit
Commit to a file now, to reveal it later.
See
.Xr noqtis-commit 1 .
.It Cm reveal
Check a file against a commitment.
See
.Xr noqtis-reveal 1 .
.It Cm cite
Print how to cite the data of a capsule.
See
.Xr noqtis-cite 1 .
.It Cm diff
Say what changed between two capsules.
See
.Xr noqtis-diff 1 .
.It Cm report
Check a capsule and write a report of it.
See
.Xr noqtis-report 1 .
.It Cm rotate , revoke
Say that a seal key was replaced, or must no longer be trusted, for
.Cm check Fl -keys .
See
.Xr noqtis-rotate 1
and
.Xr noqtis-revoke 1 .
.It Cm idcard
Make or check an identity card: a signing key with its holder's name,
ORCID iD, e-mail address, web page and affiliation, signed by the key,
for
.Cm check Fl -card .
See
.Xr noqtis-idcard 1 .
.It Cm timestamp
Add an RFC 3161 timestamp to a folder sealed before.
See
.Xr noqtis-timestamp 1 .
.It Cm ers
Start or renew a sealed folder's RFC 4998 evidence record, which keeps
its timestamp provable after the TSA's certificate expires or its hash
weakens.
See
.Xr noqtis-ers 1 .
.It Cm inspect
Show what a sealed folder says (format, algorithm, key fingerprint,
created, files, size, timestamp, meta) without checking it.
See
.Xr noqtis-inspect 1 .
.It Cm algs
List every algorithm and its sizes.
See
.Xr noqtis-algs 1 .
.It Cm selftest
Run the known-answer self-test.
See
.Xr noqtis-selftest 1 .
.It Cm version
Print the version.
See
.Xr noqtis-version 1 .
.It Cm help Op Ar command
Print the list of commands, or the options of one command.
.El
.Pp
Options follow the command.
A short option takes its value as the next argument or joined to it
.Pq Fl k Ns Ar key ;
a long option takes it as the next argument or after
.Ql =
.Pq Fl -key Ns = Ns Ar key .
.Fl h
or
.Fl -help
after any command prints its usage.
.Fl -
ends the options; an argument
.Ql -
alone is a positional argument (standard input where a command allows it).
.Ss Algorithm names
Algorithms are named as
.Cm noqtis algs
prints them, without regard to case:
.Cm ML-DSA-44 , ML-DSA-65 , ML-DSA-87 ,
.Cm SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s , SLH-DSA-SHA2-128f , SLH-DSA-SHA2-192s ,
.Cm SLH-DSA-SHA2-192f , SLH-DSA-SHA2-256s , SLH-DSA-SHA2-256f ,
.Cm SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128s , SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128f , SLH-DSA-SHAKE-192s ,
.Cm SLH-DSA-SHAKE-192f , SLH-DSA-SHAKE-256s , SLH-DSA-SHAKE-256f ,
.Cm ML-DSA-44+Ed25519 , ML-DSA-65+Ed25519 , ML-DSA-87+Ed25519
(post-quantum signatures, the last three hybrid),
.Cm Ed25519
(classical, not for seals) and
.Cm ML-KEM-512 , ML-KEM-768 , ML-KEM-1024 , HQC-128 , HQC-192 , HQC-256
(key encapsulation).
.Ss Key files
A key file is text: one header line, then the key bytes in the standard's
own encoding (FIPS 203, 204, 205; HQC v5.0.0) as base64 (RFC 4648, with
padding) in lines of 64 characters:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
noqtis-key-1 <algorithm> <pub|sec>
<base64>
\&...
.Ed
.Pp
Readers accept
.Ql \en
or
.Ql \er\en
line ends and reject any other character, non-canonical padding, a header
that is not exactly three space-separated fields, the wrong kind of key
.Po
.Ql pub
where a secret key is needed, or the reverse
.Pc ,
and a key whose decoded length is not the algorithm's size.
The base64 encoder and decoder run in constant time.
.Pp
Signatures and ciphertexts are raw bytes in the standard's encoding, with
no header.
A shared secret file holds the raw 32 bytes.
.Ss Secrets and files
Secret key and shared-secret files are created owner-only (mode 0600,
with
.Dv O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW
on POSIX; on Windows they inherit the folder's ACL) through unbuffered
streams.
No command overwrites an existing file unless given
.Fl -force .
A file a command writes (a key, a signature, a shared secret, a report,
a bundle, the output of
.Cm box ,
.Cm unbox
and the rest) is written first to a new temporary name in the same
folder,
.Pa .noqtis.<16 hex digits>.tmp ,
created with
.Dv O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW ,
and given its name only once it is whole: with
.Fl -force
the new file replaces the old one (and a link there, never its target),
so it never inherits the old one's permissions; without it, a file that
appeared under the name meanwhile is not replaced.
A command with two outputs (a key pair, a ciphertext and its shared
secret, a commitment's salt and the data record) writes both whole
before it names either, and names them one right after the other with
the signals held back.
A folder a command makes
.Pq Cm export --as bagit , Cm pack unpack
is filled under a temporary name the same way and named only when whole;
a folder is never replaced.
The data record
.Pq Pa noqtis.data.json
and the files of
.Cm data syn
are written through
.Pa .noqtis. Ns Ar pid Ns Ar k Ns Pa .tmp
(16 hex digits as well) and renamed into place.
When the command fails, or is stopped by one of the signals
.Sx SIGNALS
lists as handled (Ctrl-C, Ctrl-\e,
.Dv SIGTERM ,
a closed pipe, a write over
.Ic ulimit -f ,
and the others), the temporary file or folder is removed, so a cut output never stands
under the name asked for; a temporary file is removed through the folder
it was made in, so that holds even when the folder is renamed while the
command runs.
While a folder is filled, a signal ends the command at its next step,
once what it made is removed.
Only a signal
.Sx SIGNALS
lists as not handled
.Pq Dv SIGKILL , No a fault such as Dv SIGSEGV
or a power cut can leave a temporary name behind (on Windows, a signal
too, as an open file cannot be removed there, and a folder renamed while
the command runs); a seal never takes such a name in as data, and
.Xr noqtis-check 1
reports one it finds as a leftover.
Every buffer that held a secret key, seed or shared secret is wiped before
it is freed.
No secret is printed: a shared secret reaches standard output only with
.Fl -stdout
(in hex), and keys never do.
.Pp
On Windows, arguments are read as UTF-16 and passed on as UTF-8, and
standard input and output are in binary mode.
.Sh SIGNALS
From its first write to its last, a command that changes files
.Po
.Cm seal , pack build , data , env , commit , timestamp , ers ,
and every command that writes an output file
.Pc
handles each signal whose
default action ends the process and that can be caught:
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGINT
(Ctrl-C),
.Dv SIGQUIT
(Ctrl-\e),
.Dv SIGPIPE
(standard output or error a pipe whose reader is gone, as in
.Ql noqtis seal -r tree | head ) ,
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Dv SIGXFSZ
(a write over
.Ic ulimit -f ) ,
.Dv SIGXCPU
.Pq Ic ulimit -t ,
.Dv SIGALRM ,
.Dv SIGVTALRM ,
.Dv SIGPROF ,
.Dv SIGUSR1 ,
.Dv SIGUSR2 ,
.Dv SIGPOLL
where the system has it (the same signal as
.Dv SIGIO
on Linux), on Linux
.Dv SIGSTKFLT
and
.Dv SIGPWR ,
and the real-time signals
.Dv SIGRTMIN
to
.Dv SIGRTMAX .
On such a signal it removes its temporary files and puts back every file
it had changed (each capsule of a
.Cm seal -r
run as well), as
.Xr noqtis-seal 1
describes, and then ends by that same signal, so the exit status a shell
shows is 128 plus its number (141 for
.Dv SIGPIPE ,
131 for
.Dv SIGQUIT ) ;
the core dump of
.Dv SIGQUIT ,
.Dv SIGXCPU
and
.Dv SIGXFSZ ,
where the system makes one, goes to the folder the command started in.
A message the put-back writes to a standard error that is a closed pipe
fails without stopping it.
Once the work is done (the last capsule sealed, the outputs named), a
signal finds nothing to put back and ends the command as it would any
program: the files then hold the complete new state.
A signal that was ignored when the command started (as
.Xr nohup 1
ignores
.Dv SIGHUP ,
or a parent ignores
.Dv SIGPIPE )
stays ignored: with
.Dv SIGPIPE
ignored, a write to a closed pipe fails, the command finishes its work,
and, when standard output was that pipe, exits 2 with
.Ql cannot write to stdout .
A signal another part of the process already handles (a profiler's
.Dv SIGPROF )
is left to it.
.Pp
.Dv SIGTSTP
(Ctrl-Z),
.Dv SIGTTIN
and
.Dv SIGTTOU
stop the command, which goes on where it was at
.Dv SIGCONT ;
a signal sent while it is stopped acts once it goes on.
.Dv SIGCHLD ,
.Dv SIGCONT ,
.Dv SIGURG
and
.Dv SIGWINCH
(and
.Dv SIGIO
and
.Dv SIGINFO
on the BSDs and macOS) are ignored by default and change nothing.
.Pp
Not handled:
.Dv SIGKILL
and
.Dv SIGSTOP ,
which no program can catch, and the faults
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
.Dv SIGBUS ,
.Dv SIGFPE ,
.Dv SIGILL ,
.Dv SIGABRT ,
.Dv SIGSYS
and
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
which a bug, a debugger or the kernel raises in the middle of any step,
when the command's own state cannot be trusted to put anything back.
Like a power cut, these can leave a temporary file, a seal written but
not stamped, or some capsules of a
.Cm seal -r
run resealed and others not;
.Xr noqtis-check 1
names each such state and says what to do (sealing again repairs it).
.Pp
On Windows only
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGTERM
are handled.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width NOQTIS_PROGRESS
.It Ev NOQTIS_PROGRESS
.Cm seal ,
.Cm check
and signing with SLH-DSA draw a progress bar on standard error when it is
a terminal and the work takes more than a third of a second; the bar is
cleared when the work ends.
.Ql 0
turns it off;
.Ql 1
draws it to a standard error that is not a terminal too.
.It Ev NOQTIS_NO_UPDATE_CHECK
After a command that ended with status 0, and only when standard error is
a terminal, the tool says in one line when a newer release exists.
It asks for the page of the latest release at most once a day, with a
limit of two seconds, and keeps the answer in the cache directory of the
user
.Pq Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME , or Pa ~/.cache ; Ev LOCALAPPDATA No on Windows .
Nothing is sent but that request, and a failure is silent.
Nothing is linked for it, and the library takes no part: on Windows the
request is made by the system (WinHTTP); elsewhere by the libcurl of the
system when there is one, else by
.Xr curl 1 ;
with neither, nothing is asked.
Set to anything, this variable turns the notice off; it is also off when
.Ev CI
is set and when standard error is not a terminal.
.Cm noqtis version --check
asks at once.
.It Ev NOQTIS_UPDATE_CHECK
.Ql 0
turns that notice off, as
.Ev NOQTIS_NO_UPDATE_CHECK
does;
.Ql 1
gives it to a standard error that is not a terminal too, and when
.Ev CI
is set.
.It Ev SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
The time
.Cm seal
writes as the manifest's
.Ql created ,
for reproducible capsules (see
.Xr noqtis-seal 1 ) .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
For every command (each page gives the details):
.Bl -tag -width 3n
.It 0
Success: a signature is valid, every capsule check passed, every digest
matched.
.It 1
A verification failed: a bad or wrong-length signature, a failed capsule
check, a digest that differs or a listed file that is missing, a malformed
.Fl -check
line.
.It 2
A usage error, an unreadable or unwritable file, the wrong kind of key, or
a library error; and for every command, standard output that could not
be written (a full disk, or a closed pipe when SIGPIPE is ignored),
whatever the command found, with
.Ql cannot write to stdout
on standard error.
.It 3
A warning, from two commands only:
.Cm drift check ,
when a column warned and none failed
.Pq Xr noqtis-drift 1 ,
and
.Cm src refresh ,
when nothing changed but a source could not be reached
.Pq Xr noqtis-src 1 .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Seal a folder, check it, change a file and check again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noqtis keygen -a ML-DSA-65 -o me
ML-DSA-65 key pair: me (secret), me.pub (public)
$ noqtis seal -k me results
sealed results with ML-DSA-65
$ noqtis check -p me.pub results
\&...
results: OK (22 checks passed)
$ echo extra >> results/report.txt
$ noqtis check -p me.pub results; echo $?
\&...
FAIL  file                report.txt: size differs
\&...
results: FAILED (1 of 22 checks failed)
1
.Ed
